# Node-RED for an Aber REMOTE gateway appliance.
#
# RECONSTRUCTED. The original of this file was overwritten during Phase 4 and was never committed,
# so this is a rebuild from what bootstrap.mjs and README.md state rather than the original text.
# The reasoning below is the reasoning those files cross-reference; if you still hold the original,
# prefer it and delete this notice.
#
# =================================================================================================
# WHY THIS APPLIANCE DOES NOT FEDERATE TO SUPABASE AUTH
#
# `bootstrap.mjs` generates a settings.js with a LOCAL `adminAuth` -- one bcrypt-hashed account,
# password printed once -- and not the OAuth2+PKCE strategy the platform's own Node-RED uses. That
# is a deliberate divergence, and it is the single most important decision in this bundle.
#
# The platform's Node-RED authenticates humans against GoTrue. That works because its callback URL
# is registered in `auth.oauth_clients` by migration 0003, from one fixed `NODERED_PUBLIC_URL`. An
# appliance cannot satisfy that:
#
#   * It lives on a shopfloor subnet, usually on DHCP, so it has no stable URL to register.
#   * GoTrue matches `redirect_uri` EXACTLY. A mismatch answers `invalid redirect_uri` at
#     /oauth/authorize -- which reads as a Node-RED fault, not a registration one.
#   * Registering one client per appliance means a migration, or a dynamic-registration call, per
#     box. Widening the existing client's `redirect_uris` to cover a subnet loosens it for the
#     platform's own editor too.
#
# And the failure would land at the worst moment: SSO needs a route to the platform, so the editor
# would be unreachable exactly when the link is down -- which is when someone is standing in front
# of the box wanting to look at it.
#
# So: one local account. It needs no network, no registration, and no coordination with the
# platform. The trade is that the password is per-appliance and cannot be centrally revoked, which
# is why it is generated (never defaulted), shown once, and re-issued with
# `bootstrap.mjs --reset-admin-password` rather than being recoverable.
#
# THE DATA PATH IS UNAFFECTED. Editor login is a human question; telemetry authenticates to
# Mosquitto with the enrolled `sparkplug_id` and its own password over MQTTS. The two auth planes
# are independent, which is what makes this substitution safe.
# =================================================================================================
#
# WHY A CUSTOM IMAGE AT ALL. /data/settings.js is generated by bootstrap.mjs and lives on a volume,
# and Node resolves `require()` from the REQUIRING FILE's directory -- so a bare `require('bcryptjs')`
# in /data/settings.js searches /data/node_modules and /node_modules, never the image's
# /usr/src/node-red/node_modules. bootstrap.mjs therefore requires by ABSOLUTE PATH, and this file
# exists to guarantee those paths are present. Same idiom, same reason, as node-red/Dockerfile on the
# platform side.
#
# BUILT ON THE APPLIANCE, not pulled. About a minute on a Raspberry Pi, and it removes any need for
# the plant to reach ghcr.io -- or for this project to publish a multi-architecture image for
# hardware it has never seen. An arm64 Pi builds this file unchanged; a published amd64 image would
# not run on one at all.
#
# THE TAG IS PINNED, to the same tag the platform pins. The generated settings.js depends on
# Node-RED contracts internal enough to move between releases (see node-red/Dockerfile for the three
# of them), and an appliance is the worst place to discover a contract change: it is the hardest
# thing in the deployment to get a shell on.
FROM nodered/node-red:5.0.7

# ASSERTED AT BUILD TIME, not discovered at first login.
#
# `bcryptjs` ships with this base image and hashes the editor password. If a future base drops it,
# settings.js throws on load and Node-RED starts WITH NO adminAuth AT ALL -- an open flow editor and
# an open /flows admin API on a plant network, which is the one failure this appliance must never
# have. Node-RED does not treat an unloadable settings.js as fatal, so nothing else would say so.
#
# The credentials runtime is checked for the same reason: bootstrap.mjs uses it to encrypt the broker
# password, and its absence would mean either a crash or, worse, a plaintext credential file.
RUN node -e "require('/usr/src/node-red/node_modules/bcryptjs'); \
             require('/usr/src/node-red/node_modules/@node-red/runtime/lib/nodes/credentials.js'); \
             console.log('bcryptjs and the credentials runtime are present')"

# No CMD and no ENTRYPOINT override here: the base image's entrypoint is correct for the `node-red`
# service. docker-compose.yml overrides both for the one-shot `bootstrap` service instead -- see the
# note there about why a bare `command:` would silently start Node-RED rather than the script.
